      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and the planet’s climate. Here’s a concise breakdown of what makes water special:\n\n| Aspect | What it means |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Chemical identity** | Two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom (H₂O). |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° angle) giving it a polar, “dipole” character. |\n| **Polarity** | The oxygen atom carries a partial negative charge; the hydrogens carry partial positive charges. This polarity allows water to dissolve many ionic and polar substances. |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds (two as donor, two as acceptor). These bonds give water its high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion. |\n| **Physical states** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). The transitions are governed by temperature and pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs a lot of heat before its temperature rises, moderating climates. <br>• **High heat of vaporization** – evaporating water takes a lot of energy, enabling cooling (sweat, evaporation). <br>• **Universal solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Cellular medium** – 70–80 % of a cell’s mass is water. <br>• **Transport** – carries nutrients, gases, and waste. <br>• **Chemical reactions** – many metabolic pathways occur in aqueous environments. |\n| **Environmental role** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff. <br>• **Climate regulation** – water vapor is a greenhouse gas; oceans store and redistribute heat. |\n| **Human use** | • **Drinking, agriculture, industry, sanitation.** <br>• **Energy** – steam turbines, cooling systems. |\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that, through its polarity and hydrogen‑bonding network, exhibits extraordinary physical and chemical properties that make it indispensable for life, ecosystems, and human technology."
